</table></td>
<td id="A4.T3.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">Flag<math id="A4.T3.m16" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}^{\star}(k_{1},k_{2},\mathbb{R}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mmultiscripts><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></mmultiscripts><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{\star}(k_{1},k_{2},\mathbb{R}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A4.T3.2.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="A4.T3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\mathrm{O}(n)}{\mathrm{SO}(k_{1})\mathrm{SO}(k_{2})\mathrm{O}(n-k_{1}-k_{2})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>SO</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\mathrm{O}(n)}{\mathrm{SO}(k_{1})\mathrm{SO}(k_{2})\mathrm{O}(n-k_{1}-k_{2})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A4.T3.2.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="A4.T3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{subarray}{c}(k_{1}+k_{2})n\\
-k_{1}^{2}-k_{2}^{2}-k_{1}k_{2}\end{subarray}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{subarray}{c}(k_{1}+k_{2})n\\
-k_{1}^{2}-k_{2}^{2}-k_{1}k_{2}\end{subarray}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A4.T3.2.6.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">Flag (Vec)</td>
<td id="A4.T3.2.6.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="A4.T3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\binom{n}{k_{1}}\binom{n}{k_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\binom{n}{k_{1}}\binom{n}{k_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="A4.T3.2.7" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A4.T3.2.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t">
<table id="A4.T3.2.7.1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="A4.T3.2.7.1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A4.T3.2.7.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">Pauli</td></tr>
<tr id="A4.T3.2.7.1.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A4.T3.2.7.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">quotient</td></tr>
</table></td>
<td id="A4.T3.2.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><math id="A4.T3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\mathrm{U}(n)}{\mathcal{P}_{n}^{\star}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\mathrm{U}(n)}{\mathcal{P}_{n}^{\star}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A4.T3.2.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><math id="A4.T3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\mathrm{U}(n)}{\mathcal{P}_{n}^{\star}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\mathrm{U}(n)}{\mathcal{P}_{n}^{\star}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A4.T3.2.7.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><math id="A4.T3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\scriptstyle n^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">n</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\scriptstyle n^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A4.T3.2.7.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t">Pauli</td>
<td id="A4.T3.2.7.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><math id="A4.T3.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\scriptstyle 2n^{4}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn mathsize="0.700em">2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">n</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\scriptstyle 2n^{4}}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>
Table listing manifold families used in the QuIIEst benchmark, their mathematical symbols, their equivalent quotient/homogeneous spaces, and their intrinsic dimensions <math id="A4.T3.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here, <math id="A4.T3.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{U}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{U}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.T3.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{O}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{O}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the unitary and orthogonal groups in <math id="A4.T3.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> dimensions, respectively, <math id="A4.T3.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SO}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SO}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the special unitary groups, and the group <math id="A4.T3.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\cal P}_{n}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒫</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\cal P}_{n}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined in Sec. <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Detailed theory for QuIIEst Manifolds ‣ Quantum-inspired Benchmark for Estimating Intrinsic Dimension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>.
The parameter <math id="A4.T3.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> for all rows except the last one, where it is assumed to be prime.
The last two columns list the six QuIIEst embeddings and their lowest possible ambient dimensions, <math id="A4.T3.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{a}^{\text{min}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>a</mi><mtext>min</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{a}^{\text{min}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Any embedding into a given ambient dimension can be further embedded into a space of larger ambient dimension via any isometry.
The use of slightly different quotients for our two Grassmanian embeddings yields a lower possible ambient dimension for the latter embedding while maintaining the same intrinsic dimension.
Note that the usual flag manifold can be obtained from Flag<math id="A4.T3.m43" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}^{\star}(k_{1},k_{2},\mathbb{R}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mmultiscripts><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></mmultiscripts><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{\star}(k_{1},k_{2},\mathbb{R}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> by letting <math id="A4.T3.m44" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SO}\to\mathrm{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SO}\to\mathrm{O}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The symbol <math id="A4.T3.m45" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\choose b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\choose b</annotation></semantics></math> is the binomial coefficient.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Throughout this section, let <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{\ket{1},\dots,\ket{n}\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{\ket{1},\dots,\ket{n}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math> be the standard (unit-vector) basis of <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Furthermore, for a matrix <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, define <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{X}</annotation></semantics></math> to be the vectorization of <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> – that is, the vector obtained by stacking the columns.</p>
</div>
<section id="A4.SS2.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Stiefel manifolds and the “St (Matrix)” embedding</h4>

<div id="A4.SS2.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Stiefel manifold is <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(k,\mathbb{R}^{n})=\left\{X\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times k}\mid X^{T}X=\mathbb{I}\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝕀</mi></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(k,\mathbb{R}^{n})=\left\{X\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times k}\mid X^{T}X=\mathbb{I}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">James (1977)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bendokat et al. (2020)</a></cite>.
This easily embeds into <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{nk}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{nk}</annotation></semantics></math> via the map <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\mapsto\vec{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\mapsto\vec{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This yields the “St (Matrix)” embedding in the QuIIEst dataset.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.SSSx1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A more interesting embedding will be used as an intermediate step to derive the Grassmanian embedding used in QuIIEst and is as follows.
We will view <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(k,\mathbb{R}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(k,\mathbb{R}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{O}(n)/\mathrm{O}(n-k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{O}(n)/\mathrm{O}(n-k)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then a point <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathrm{St}(k,\mathbb{R}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathrm{St}(k,\mathbb{R}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> is given by the first <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> columns of the corresponding orthogonal matrix.
Using the notation from Section <a href="#A4.SS1" title="D.1 Gilmore-Perelomov embeddings ‣ Appendix D Detailed theory for QuIIEst Manifolds ‣ Quantum-inspired Benchmark for Estimating Intrinsic Dimension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.1</span></a>,
we let <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}=\mathrm{O}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}=\mathrm{O}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}=\mathrm{O}(n-k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}=\mathrm{O}(n-k)</annotation></semantics></math>, and
define the representation <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi\colon\mathcal{O}\mapsto\mathcal{O}^{\otimes k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi\colon\mathcal{O}\mapsto\mathcal{O}^{\otimes k}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}\in\mathrm{O}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}\in\mathrm{O}(n)</annotation></semantics></math>.
An obvious choice for the state <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ket{\mathcal{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ket{\mathcal{H}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ket{1}\otimes\dots\otimes\ket{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ket{1}\otimes\dots\otimes\ket{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Given <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}\in\mathrm{O}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}\in\mathrm{O}(n)</annotation></semantics></math>, this yields the embedding</p>
<table id="A4.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A4.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ket{\psi_{\mathrm{St}}(\mathcal{O})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>St</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ket{\psi_{\mathrm{St}}(\mathcal{O})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\Pi(\mathcal{O})\ket{\mathcal{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\Pi(\mathcal{O})\ket{\mathcal{H}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A4.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathcal{O}\ket{1}\otimes\mathcal{O}\ket{2}\otimes\dots\otimes\mathcal{O}\ket{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mn>2</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathcal{O}\ket{1}\otimes\mathcal{O}\ket{2}\otimes\dots\otimes\mathcal{O}\ket{k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A4.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{j_{1},\dots,j_{k}=1}^{n}\mathcal{O}_{j_{1},1}\dots\mathcal{O}_{j_{k},k}\ket{j_{1}}\otimes\dots\otimes\ket{j_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{j_{1},\dots,j_{k}=1}^{n}\mathcal{O}_{j_{1},1}\dots\mathcal{O}_{j_{k},k}\ket{j_{1}}\otimes\dots\otimes\ket{j_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A4.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\ket{\mathcal{O}_{\ast,1}}\otimes\dots\otimes\ket{\mathcal{O}_{\ast,k}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\ket{\mathcal{O}_{\ast,1}}\otimes\dots\otimes\ket{\mathcal{O}_{\ast,k}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS2.SSSx1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ket{\mathcal{O}_{\ast,i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ket{\mathcal{O}_{\ast,i}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{\rm th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>i</mi><mi>th</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{\rm th}</annotation></semantics></math> column of <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}</annotation></semantics></math>.
From this we can easily see that <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ket{\psi_{\mathrm{St}}(\mathcal{O})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>St</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ket{\psi_{\mathrm{St}}(\mathcal{O})}</annotation></semantics></math> depends only on the first <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> columns of <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}</annotation></semantics></math>, but will result in a different vector for different elements of the Stiefel manifold.
It is therefore an injection of the Stiefel manifold into <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{n^{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{n^{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Grassmannians and the “Gr (Vec)” embedding</h4>

<div id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the embedding <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ket{\psi_{\mathrm{St}}(\mathcal{O})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>St</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ket{\psi_{\mathrm{St}}(\mathcal{O})}</annotation></semantics></math> of the Stiefel manifold from above.
We need to slightly modify this to get an embedding of the Grassmannian,</p>
<table id="A4.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Gr}^{\star}(k,\mathbb{R}^{n})\cong\frac{\mathrm{St}(k,\mathbb{R}^{n})}{\mathrm{SO}(k)}\cong\frac{\mathrm{O}(n)}{\mathrm{O}(n-k)\times\mathrm{SO}(k)}\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Gr</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>≅</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Gr}^{\star}(k,\mathbb{R}^{n})\cong\frac{\mathrm{St}(k,\mathbb{R}^{n})}{\mathrm{SO}(k)}\cong\frac{\mathrm{O}(n)}{\mathrm{O}(n-k)\times\mathrm{SO}(k)}\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">due to the extra quotient by <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SO}(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SO}(k)</annotation></semantics></math>.
In particular, we define</p>
<table id="A4.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ket{\mathcal{H}}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{k!}}\sum_{\sigma\in S_{k}}\text{sgn}(\sigma)\ket{\sigma(1)}\otimes\dots\otimes\ket{\sigma(k)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></msqrt></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mrow><mtext>sgn</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ket{\mathcal{H}}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{k!}}\sum_{\sigma\in S_{k}}\text{sgn}(\sigma)\ket{\sigma(1)}\otimes\dots\otimes\ket{\sigma(k)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{sgn}(\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>sgn</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{sgn}(\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the symmetric group on <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> elements.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We note that Eq. equation <a href="#A4.E6" title="In Grassmannians and the “Gr (Vec)” embedding ‣ D.2 Embedding manifolds into Euclidean space ‣ Appendix D Detailed theory for QuIIEst Manifolds ‣ Quantum-inspired Benchmark for Estimating Intrinsic Dimension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> is not the usual definition of the Grassmannian.
Indeed, Grassmannian is typically <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Gr}(k,\mathbb{R}^{n})=\frac{\mathrm{O}(n)}{\mathrm{O}(n-k)\times\mathrm{O}(k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Gr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Gr}(k,\mathbb{R}^{n})=\frac{\mathrm{O}(n)}{\mathrm{O}(n-k)\times\mathrm{O}(k)}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the oriented Grassmannian is <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathrm{Gr}}(k,\mathbb{R}^{n})=\frac{\mathrm{SO}(n)}{\mathrm{SO}(n-k)\times\mathrm{SO}(k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Gr</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathrm{Gr}}(k,\mathbb{R}^{n})=\frac{\mathrm{SO}(n)}{\mathrm{SO}(n-k)\times\mathrm{SO}(k)}</annotation></semantics></math>.
These two spaces, along with the space in Eq. equation <a href="#A4.E6" title="In Grassmannians and the “Gr (Vec)” embedding ‣ D.2 Embedding manifolds into Euclidean space ‣ Appendix D Detailed theory for QuIIEst Manifolds ‣ Quantum-inspired Benchmark for Estimating Intrinsic Dimension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, all have the same ID.
In future work, we will add the standard and oriented Grassmannian to QuIIEst and further compare the performance of standard ID estimation methods on these three very similar manifolds.
In particular, the geometry and topology of these three manifolds are slightly different despite the manifolds themselves being morally very similar, thus yielding an interesting testing ground.
For example, we list the first two homotopy groups of these manifolds in Table. <a href="#A4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ Grassmannians and the “Gr (Vec)” embedding ‣ D.2 Embedding manifolds into Euclidean space ‣ Appendix D Detailed theory for QuIIEst Manifolds ‣ Quantum-inspired Benchmark for Estimating Intrinsic Dimension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, which we calulate using the long exact sequence of homotopy groups induced from the fiber bundle <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}\to\mathcal{G}\to\mathcal{G}/\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>/</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}\to\mathcal{G}\to\mathcal{G}/\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.T4" class="ltx_table">
