<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span>The first two homotopy groups of the three types of “Grassmannians” described below Eq. equation <a href="#A4.E6" title="In Grassmannians and the “Gr (Vec)” embedding ‣ D.2 Embedding manifolds into Euclidean space ‣ Appendix D Detailed theory for QuIIEst Manifolds ‣ Quantum-inspired Benchmark for Estimating Intrinsic Dimension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Given <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>, we achieve the embedding</p>
<table id="A4.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A4.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ket{\psi_{\mathrm{Gr}}(\mathcal{O})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>Gr</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ket{\psi_{\mathrm{Gr}}(\mathcal{O})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\Pi(\mathcal{O})\ket{\mathcal{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\Pi(\mathcal{O})\ket{\mathcal{H}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A4.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{\sqrt{k!}}\sum_{\sigma\in S_{k}}\text{sgn}(\sigma)\mathcal{O}\ket{\sigma(1)}\otimes\dots\otimes\mathcal{O}\ket{\sigma(k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mtext>sgn</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{\sqrt{k!}}\sum_{\sigma\in S_{k}}\text{sgn}(\sigma)\mathcal{O}\ket{\sigma(1)}\otimes\dots\otimes\mathcal{O}\ket{\sigma(k)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A4.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{\sqrt{k!}}\sum_{\sigma\in S_{k}}\sum_{j_{1},\dots,j_{k}=1}^{n}\text{sgn}(\sigma)\mathcal{O}_{j_{1},\sigma(1)}\dots\mathcal{O}_{j_{k},\sigma(k)}\ket{j_{1}}\otimes\dots\otimes\ket{j_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mtext>sgn</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{\sqrt{k!}}\sum_{\sigma\in S_{k}}\sum_{j_{1},\dots,j_{k}=1}^{n}\text{sgn}(\sigma)\mathcal{O}_{j_{1},\sigma(1)}\dots\mathcal{O}_{j_{k},\sigma(k)}\ket{j_{1}}\otimes\dots\otimes\ket{j_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A4.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{\sqrt{k!}}\sum_{j_{1},\dots,j_{k}=1}^{n}\det(\mathcal{O}_{\left(j_{1},\dots,j_{k}\right)})\ket{j_{1}}\otimes\dots\otimes\ket{j_{k}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{\sqrt{k!}}\sum_{j_{1},\dots,j_{k}=1}^{n}\det(\mathcal{O}_{\left(j_{1},\dots,j_{k}\right)})\ket{j_{1}}\otimes\dots\otimes\ket{j_{k}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\left(j_{1},\dots,j_{k}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\left(j_{1},\dots,j_{k}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\times k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\times k</annotation></semantics></math> matrix obtained from <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}</annotation></semantics></math> by taking the first <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> columns and taking the rows <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{1},\dots,j_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{1},\dots,j_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and similarly
<math id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\left\{j_{1},\dots,j_{k}\right\}}=\mathcal{O}_{\operatorname{sorted}\left(j_{1},\dots,j_{k}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>}</mo></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>sorted</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\left\{j_{1},\dots,j_{k}\right\}}=\mathcal{O}_{\operatorname{sorted}\left(j_{1},\dots,j_{k}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in S_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in S_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> be the permutation that sorts <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(j_{1},\dots,j_{k}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(j_{1},\dots,j_{k}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\det\mathcal{O}_{\left(j_{1},\dots,j_{k}\right)}=\text{sgn}(\sigma)\det\mathcal{O}_{\left\{j_{1},\dots,j_{k}\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">det</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>sgn</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">det</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>}</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\det\mathcal{O}_{\left(j_{1},\dots,j_{k}\right)}=\text{sgn}(\sigma)\det\mathcal{O}_{\left\{j_{1},\dots,j_{k}\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore, we have that</p>
<table id="A4.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A4.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ket{\psi_{\mathrm{Gr}}(\mathcal{O})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>Gr</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ket{\psi_{\mathrm{Gr}}(\mathcal{O})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}Q\subset\left\{1,\dots,n\right\}\\
\left\lvert Q\right\rvert=k\end{subarray}}\det\mathcal{O}_{Q}\frac{1}{\sqrt{k!}}\sum_{\sigma\in S_{k}}\text{sgn}(\sigma)\ket{\sigma(Q_{1})}\otimes\dots\otimes\ket{\sigma(Q_{k})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder></mstyle><mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mtext>sgn</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}Q\subset\left\{1,\dots,n\right\}\\
\left\lvert Q\right\rvert=k\end{subarray}}\det\mathcal{O}_{Q}\frac{1}{\sqrt{k!}}\sum_{\sigma\in S_{k}}\text{sgn}(\sigma)\ket{\sigma(Q_{1})}\otimes\dots\otimes\ket{\sigma(Q_{k})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A4.E13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}Q\subset\left\{1,\dots,n\right\}\\
\left\lvert Q\right\rvert=k\end{subarray}}\det\mathcal{O}_{Q}\ket{Q}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder></mstyle><mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}Q\subset\left\{1,\dots,n\right\}\\
\left\lvert Q\right\rvert=k\end{subarray}}\det\mathcal{O}_{Q}\ket{Q},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where we defined <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to be the <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{\rm th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>i</mi><mi>th</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{\rm th}</annotation></semantics></math> element of the set <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> (of course this does not make sense generally, but because we are summing over all permutations, it is fine to pick some arbitrary ordering of the set),
and we defined the state</p>
<table id="A4.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ket{Q}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{k!}}\sum_{\sigma\in S_{k}}\text{sgn}(\sigma)\ket{\sigma(Q_{1})}\otimes\dots\otimes\ket{\sigma(Q_{k})}~." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></msqrt></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mrow><mtext>sgn</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ket{Q}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{k!}}\sum_{\sigma\in S_{k}}\text{sgn}(\sigma)\ket{\sigma(Q_{1})}\otimes\dots\otimes\ket{\sigma(Q_{k})}~.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Notice that we are embedding into <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{n^{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{n^{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>, but a full basis for the space is given by <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ket{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ket{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\subset\left\{1,\dots,n\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\subset\left\{1,\dots,n\right\}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lvert Q\right\rvert=k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lvert Q\right\rvert=k</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, this embedding in general gives us an embedding into <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d_{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d_{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{A}\geq\binom{n}{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{A}\geq\binom{n}{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We denote this by the shorthand “Gr (Vec)” in Table <a href="#A4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ D.2 Embedding manifolds into Euclidean space ‣ Appendix D Detailed theory for QuIIEst Manifolds ‣ Quantum-inspired Benchmark for Estimating Intrinsic Dimension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We note that this embedding is almost the Plücker embedding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bendokat et al. (2020)</a></cite>.
The Plücker embedding is an embedding of the standard Grassmanian into real projective space, which is the real sphere with antipodal points identified.
Above, we are embedding Eq. equation <a href="#A4.E6" title="In Grassmannians and the “Gr (Vec)” embedding ‣ D.2 Embedding manifolds into Euclidean space ‣ Appendix D Detailed theory for QuIIEst Manifolds ‣ Quantum-inspired Benchmark for Estimating Intrinsic Dimension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> into the real sphere.
The reason that <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{\mathrm{Gr}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>Gr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{\mathrm{Gr}}</annotation></semantics></math> is not an embedding of <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{O}(n)/\mathrm{O}(n-k)\times\mathrm{O}(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{O}(n)/\mathrm{O}(n-k)\times\mathrm{O}(k)</annotation></semantics></math> is because the vector <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ket{\mathcal{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ket{\mathcal{H}}</annotation></semantics></math> is not invariant under the action <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{O}(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{O}(k)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Instead, it is invariant under the action of <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SO}(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SO}(k)</annotation></semantics></math>, and yields a <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 1</annotation></semantics></math> phase factor under the action of <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{O}(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{O}(k)</annotation></semantics></math>.
This yields a well-defined embedding of <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{O}(n)/\mathrm{O}(n-k)\times\mathrm{O}(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{O}(n)/\mathrm{O}(n-k)\times\mathrm{O}(k)</annotation></semantics></math> into projective space, but further embedding projective space into the sphere via the standard maps (e.g., <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{i})\mapsto(x_{i}x_{j})_{i\leq j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{i})\mapsto(x_{i}x_{j})_{i\leq j}</annotation></semantics></math> for projective-space vectors <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx2.p6.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(\cdots x_{j}\cdots)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\cdots x_{j}\cdots)</annotation></semantics></math>) would come at a price of quadratically increasing the lowest possible ambient dimension <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Simanca (2018)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">permutation_matrix (https://math.stackexchange.com/users/913340/permutation matrix)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">reuns (https://math.stackexchange.com/users/276986/reuns)</a></cite>.
We thus stick with our original mapping in order to be able to run smaller-scale numerical experiments.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2.SSSx3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Stiefel manifold revisited: the “St (Vec)” embedding</h4>

<div id="A4.SS2.SSSx3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A point in the Stiefel manifold can be represented by a point on the Grassmannian and a matrix <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}\in\mathrm{SO}(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}\in\mathrm{SO}(k)</annotation></semantics></math>.
In other words, any element of <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{O}(n)/\mathrm{O}(n-k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{O}(n)/\mathrm{O}(n-k)</annotation></semantics></math> can be expressed as an element of <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{O}(n)/\mathrm{O}(n-k)\times\mathrm{SO}(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{O}(n)/\mathrm{O}(n-k)\times\mathrm{SO}(k)</annotation></semantics></math> and an element of <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SO}(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SO}(k)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore, given an element <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{O},\mathcal{V})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{O},\mathcal{V})</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(k,\mathbb{R}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(k,\mathbb{R}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}\in\mathrm{O}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}\in\mathrm{O}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> represents a point on <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Gr}^{\star}(k,\mathbb{R}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Gr</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Gr}^{\star}(k,\mathbb{R}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}\in\mathrm{SO}(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}\in\mathrm{SO}(k)</annotation></semantics></math>, we can define an embedding</p>
<table id="A4.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ket{\tilde{\psi}_{\mathrm{St}}}=\ket{\psi_{\mathrm{Gr}}(\mathcal{O})}\oplus\vec{\mathcal{V}}~," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ψ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>St</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>Gr</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ket{\tilde{\psi}_{\mathrm{St}}}=\ket{\psi_{\mathrm{Gr}}(\mathcal{O})}\oplus\vec{\mathcal{V}}~,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS2.SSSx3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">recalling that <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{\mathcal{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{\mathcal{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the vectorized matrix <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This embedding has dimension <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d+k^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d+k^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq\binom{n}{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq\binom{n}{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, and we denote this by “St (Vec)”.
This is much less than the dimension of the embedding <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{\mathrm{St}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>St</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{\mathrm{St}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.SSSx3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSSx3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To generate random points on the Stiefel manifold with this embedding, we can just generate a random <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}\in\mathrm{O}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}\in\mathrm{O}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> and a random <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}\in\mathrm{SO}(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}\in\mathrm{SO}(k)</annotation></semantics></math> and then construct the embedding.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2.SSSx4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">The “Gr (Proj)” embedding</h4>

<div id="A4.SS2.SSSx4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSSx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Gr}(k,\mathbb{R}^{n})=\mathrm{O}(n)/\mathrm{O}(n-k)\times\mathrm{O}(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Gr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Gr}(k,\mathbb{R}^{n})=\mathrm{O}(n)/\mathrm{O}(n-k)\times\mathrm{O}(k)</annotation></semantics></math> is the manifold of <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional subspaces of <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, we can uniquely represent a point on this manifold by a projector that projects onto this corresponding subspace.
In particular, given an <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times k</annotation></semantics></math> orthogonal matrix <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O},\mathcal{O}^{T}\mathcal{O}=\mathbb{I}_{k\times k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝕀</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O},\mathcal{O}^{T}\mathcal{O}=\mathbb{I}_{k\times k}</annotation></semantics></math> representing a point on <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{St}(k,\mathbb{R}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>St</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{St}(k,\mathbb{R}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, we can create the projector <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\mathcal{O}}=\mathcal{O}\mathcal{O}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\mathcal{O}}=\mathcal{O}\mathcal{O}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> that projects onto the span of the columns of <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}</annotation></semantics></math>.
From this projector, we define the “Gr (Proj)” embedding as its vectorization <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{P}_{\mathcal{O}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{P}_{\mathcal{O}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.SSSx4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSSx4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As is, the Gr (Vec) and Gr (Proj) embed different spaces — <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{O}(n)/\mathrm{O}(n-k)\times\mathrm{SO}(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{O}(n)/\mathrm{O}(n-k)\times\mathrm{SO}(k)</annotation></semantics></math> versus <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{O}(n)/\mathrm{O}(n-k)\times\mathrm{O}(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{O}(n)/\mathrm{O}(n-k)\times\mathrm{O}(k)</annotation></semantics></math> — but the intrinsic dimension remains the same.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2.SSSx5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Flag manifolds and the “Flag (Vec)” embedding</h4>

<div id="A4.SS2.SSSx5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSSx5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we consider a general flag manifold <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\mathrm{O}(n)}{\mathrm{SO}(k_{1})\times\dots\times\mathrm{SO}(k_{t})\times\mathrm{O}(n-k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\mathrm{O}(n)}{\mathrm{SO}(k_{1})\times\dots\times\mathrm{SO}(k_{t})\times\mathrm{O}(n-k)}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{t}k_{i}=k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{t}k_{i}=k</annotation></semantics></math>.
We note, as with the Grassmannians, that our definition of the Flag manifolds also slightly differs from the standard. Namely,
the typical Flag manifold is
<math id="A4.SS2.SSSx5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\mathrm{O}(n)}{\mathrm{O}(k_{1})\times\dots\times\mathrm{O}(k_{t})\times\mathrm{O}(n-k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\mathrm{O}(n)}{\mathrm{O}(k_{1})\times\dots\times\mathrm{O}(k_{t})\times\mathrm{O}(n-k)}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We will add these to QuIIEst in future work.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.SSSx5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSSx5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We begin with the case <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=2</annotation></semantics></math>, as presented in Table <a href="#A4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ D.2 Embedding manifolds into Euclidean space ‣ Appendix D Detailed theory for QuIIEst Manifolds ‣ Quantum-inspired Benchmark for Estimating Intrinsic Dimension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
Again, in the notation of Section <a href="#A4.SS1" title="D.1 Gilmore-Perelomov embeddings ‣ Appendix D Detailed theory for QuIIEst Manifolds ‣ Quantum-inspired Benchmark for Estimating Intrinsic Dimension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.1</span></a>, we work with the representation <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi\colon\mathcal{O}\mapsto\mathcal{O}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi\colon\mathcal{O}\mapsto\mathcal{O}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, and we use the fiducial vector</p>
<table id="A4.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ket{\mathcal{H}}=\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{k_{1}!}}\sum_{\sigma\in S_{k_{1}}}\ket{\sigma(1)}\otimes\dots\otimes\ket{\sigma(k_{1})}\right)\otimes\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{k_{2}!}}\sum_{\sigma\in S_{k_{2}}}\ket{\sigma(k_{1}+1)}\otimes\dots\otimes\ket{\sigma(k_{1}+k_{2})}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>!</mo></mrow></msqrt></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>!</mo></mrow></msqrt></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ket{\mathcal{H}}=\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{k_{1}!}}\sum_{\sigma\in S_{k_{1}}}\ket{\sigma(1)}\otimes\dots\otimes\ket{\sigma(k_{1})}\right)\otimes\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{k_{2}!}}\sum_{\sigma\in S_{k_{2}}}\ket{\sigma(k_{1}+1)}\otimes\dots\otimes\ket{\sigma(k_{1}+k_{2})}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS2.SSSx5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">This yields the embedding</p>
<table id="A4.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A4.E17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ket{\psi_{F}(\mathcal{O})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ket{\psi_{F}(\mathcal{O})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\Pi(\mathcal{O})\ket{\mathcal{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\Pi(\mathcal{O})\ket{\mathcal{H}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A4.E18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle={\scriptscriptstyle\frac{1}{\sqrt{k_{1}!k_{2}!}}}\sum_{j_{1},\dots,j_{k}=1}^{n}{\scriptstyle\det\!\left(\mathcal{O}_{(j_{1},\dots,j_{k_{1}}),(1,\dots,k_{1})}\right)\det\!\left(\mathcal{O}_{(j_{k_{1}+1},\dots,j_{k}),(k_{1}+1,\dots,k)}\right)\ket{j_{1}}\otimes\dots\otimes\ket{j_{k}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle scriptlevel="+2"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.227em;" width="1.227em"><mo lspace="0.167em" mathsize="0.700em">det</mo></mpadded><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒪</mi><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.710em">j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.710em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.710em" mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.710em">j</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">)</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.710em">,</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">(</mo><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.710em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.710em" mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.710em">k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.227em;" width="1.227em"><mo mathsize="0.700em">det</mo></mpadded><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒪</mi><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.710em">j</mi><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo mathsize="0.710em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.710em" mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.710em">j</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">)</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.710em">,</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.710em">k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.710em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></mrow><mo mathsize="0.710em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.710em" mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.710em">k</mi><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo fence="false" maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">j</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo fence="false" maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">j</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle={\scriptscriptstyle\frac{1}{\sqrt{k_{1}!k_{2}!}}}\sum_{j_{1},\dots,j_{k}=1}^{n}{\scriptstyle\det\!\left(\mathcal{O}_{(j_{1},\dots,j_{k_{1}}),(1,\dots,k_{1})}\right)\det\!\left(\mathcal{O}_{(j_{k_{1}+1},\dots,j_{k}),(k_{1}+1,\dots,k)}\right)\ket{j_{1}}\otimes\dots\otimes\ket{j_{k}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A4.E19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E19.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle={\scriptstyle\frac{1}{\sqrt{k_{1}!k_{2}!}}}\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}Q\subset\left\{1,\dots,n\right\}\\
\left\lvert Q\right\rvert=k_{1}\end{subarray}}\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}P\subset\left\{1,\dots,n\right\}\\
\left\lvert P\right\rvert=k_{2}\end{subarray}}\sum_{\sigma\in S_{k_{1}}}\sum_{\pi\in S_{k_{2}}}\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle scriptlevel="+1"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></msub><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></msub><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></mrow></msub><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle={\scriptstyle\frac{1}{\sqrt{k_{1}!k_{2}!}}}\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}Q\subset\left\{1,\dots,n\right\}\\
\left\lvert Q\right\rvert=k_{1}\end{subarray}}\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}P\subset\left\{1,\dots,n\right\}\\
\left\lvert P\right\rvert=k_{2}\end{subarray}}\sum_{\sigma\in S_{k_{1}}}\sum_{\pi\in S_{k_{2}}}\times</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A4.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\scriptstyle\hskip 8.19447pt\times\text{sgn}(\sigma)\text{sgn}(\pi)\det\!\left(\mathcal{O}_{Q,(1,\dots,k_{1})}\right)\det\!\left(\mathcal{O}_{P,(k_{1}+1,\dots,k)}\right)\ket{\sigma(Q_{1})}\otimes\dots\otimes\ket{\sigma(Q_{k_{1}})}\otimes\ket{\pi(P_{1})}\otimes\dots\otimes\ket{\pi(P_{k_{2}})}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="1.042em" mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext mathsize="0.700em">sgn</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">σ</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext mathsize="0.700em">sgn</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">π</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.227em;" width="1.227em"><mo mathsize="0.700em">det</mo></mpadded><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em">Q</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">,</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">(</mo><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.710em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.710em" mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.710em">k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.227em;" width="1.227em"><mo mathsize="0.700em">det</mo></mpadded><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em">P</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">,</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.710em">k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.710em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></mrow><mo mathsize="0.710em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.710em" mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.710em">k</mi><mo maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo fence="false" maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">Q</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo fence="false" maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">Q</mi><msub><mi mathsize="0.710em">k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo fence="false" maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">P</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo fence="false" maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">P</mi><msub><mi mathsize="0.710em">k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\scriptstyle\hskip 8.19447pt\times\text{sgn}(\sigma)\text{sgn}(\pi)\det\!\left(\mathcal{O}_{Q,(1,\dots,k_{1})}\right)\det\!\left(\mathcal{O}_{P,(k_{1}+1,\dots,k)}\right)\ket{\sigma(Q_{1})}\otimes\dots\otimes\ket{\sigma(Q_{k_{1}})}\otimes\ket{\pi(P_{1})}\otimes\dots\otimes\ket{\pi(P_{k_{2}})}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A4.E20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}Q\subset\left\{1,\dots,n\right\}\\
\left\lvert Q\right\rvert=k_{1}\end{subarray}}\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}P\subset\left\{1,\dots,n\right\}\\
\left\lvert P\right\rvert=k_{2}\end{subarray}}\det\!\left(\mathcal{O}_{Q,\left\{1,\dots,k_{1}\right\}}\right)\det\!\left(\mathcal{O}_{P,\left\{k_{1}+1,\dots,k\right\}}\right)\ket{Q}\otimes\ket{P}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder></mstyle><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.177em;" width="1.177em"><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false">det</mo></mpadded><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.177em;" width="1.177em"><mo movablelimits="false">det</mo></mpadded><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}Q\subset\left\{1,\dots,n\right\}\\
\left\lvert Q\right\rvert=k_{1}\end{subarray}}\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}P\subset\left\{1,\dots,n\right\}\\
\left\lvert P\right\rvert=k_{2}\end{subarray}}\det\!\left(\mathcal{O}_{Q,\left\{1,\dots,k_{1}\right\}}\right)\det\!\left(\mathcal{O}_{P,\left\{k_{1}+1,\dots,k\right\}}\right)\ket{Q}\otimes\ket{P}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.SSSx5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSSx5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As is, this embedding is into <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{n^{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{n^{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>, but because an orthonormal basis is given by tensor products of <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ket{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ket{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, we see that this embedding works into <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq\binom{n}{k_{1}}\binom{n}{k_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq\binom{n}{k_{1}}\binom{n}{k_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We call this the “Flag (Vec)” embedding.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.SSSx5.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSSx5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">A straightforward extension to general <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> yields an embedding of <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\mathrm{O}(n)}{\mathrm{SO}(k_{1})\times\dots\times\mathrm{SO}(k_{t})\times\mathrm{O}(n-k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\mathrm{O}(n)}{\mathrm{SO}(k_{1})\times\dots\times\mathrm{SO}(k_{t})\times\mathrm{O}(n-k)}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> for any ambient dimension <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq\prod_{i=1}^{t}\binom{n}{k_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">≥</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq\prod_{i=1}^{t}\binom{n}{k_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2.SSSx6" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">The “Pauli” embedding</h4>

<div id="A4.SS2.SSSx6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSSx6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We would like to construct an embedding for the quotient space <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}/\mathcal{H}=\mathrm{U}(n)/{\cal P}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>/</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>𝒫</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}/\mathcal{H}=\mathrm{U}(n)/{\cal P}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{n}=\langle e^{i\frac{2\pi}{n}},X,Z\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{n}=\langle e^{i\frac{2\pi}{n}},X,Z\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> is
the Pauli (a.k.a. Heisenberg-Weyl) group of prime dimension <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>,
and where <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> and the real-valued <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> are the standard <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional
qudit Pauli matrices <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bittel et al. (2025)</a></cite>. To construct the quotient space
using the Gilmore-Perelomov prescription in Section <a href="#A4.SS1" title="D.1 Gilmore-Perelomov embeddings ‣ Appendix D Detailed theory for QuIIEst Manifolds ‣ Quantum-inspired Benchmark for Estimating Intrinsic Dimension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.1</span></a>, we require a vector <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ket{\mathcal{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ket{\mathcal{H}}</annotation></semantics></math> in some
representation of <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{U}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{U}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> that is invariant under <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\cal P}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒫</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\cal P}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>
and not invariant under any <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{U}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{U}(n)</annotation></semantics></math>-subgroup that contains <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\cal P}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒫</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\cal P}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.SSSx6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSSx6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We pick the <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{4}</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional four-fold tensor-product unitary representation
<math id="A4.SS2.SSSx6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi\colon U\mapsto U\otimes\overline{U}\otimes U\otimes\overline{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi\colon U\mapsto U\otimes\overline{U}\otimes U\otimes\overline{U}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{U}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{U}(n)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The corresponding Pauli representation is then <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\otimes\overline{Z}\otimes Z\otimes\overline{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\otimes\overline{Z}\otimes Z\otimes\overline{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\otimes X\otimes X\otimes X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\otimes X\otimes X\otimes X</annotation></semantics></math>, where we recall that <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx6.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is
real. There is an <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx6.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional subspace <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx6.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> that is invariant
under this Pauli representation. It is spanned by the vectors</p>
<table id="A4.E21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ket{\overline{a,b}}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{n}}\sum_{c\in\mathbb{Z}_{n}}\ket{c,c+a,c+b,c+a+b}\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ket{\overline{a,b}}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{n}}\sum_{c\in\mathbb{Z}_{n}}\ket{c,c+a,c+b,c+a+b}\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS2.SSSx6.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx6.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b\in\mathbb{Z}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b\in\mathbb{Z}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, and where addition inside the kets is done modulo <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx6.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Our “Pauli”
embedding is constructed
by defining <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx6.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ket{\mathcal{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ket{\mathcal{H}}</annotation></semantics></math> to be a random unit vector in <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx6.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, as in Section <a href="#A4.SS1" title="D.1 Gilmore-Perelomov embeddings ‣ Appendix D Detailed theory for QuIIEst Manifolds ‣ Quantum-inspired Benchmark for Estimating Intrinsic Dimension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.1</span></a>,
we apply
a the unitary rotation in the four-fold tensor-product representation,
and embed the resulting <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx6.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{4}</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional complex vector into
<math id="A4.SS2.SSSx6.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{2n^{4}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{2n^{4}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.SSSx6.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSSx6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We now narrow down the quotient space that is spanned by our “Pauli”
embedding. It has also been shown Ref. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bittel et al. (2025)</a></cite> that <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is not
invariant under the larger Clifford group <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{n}\supseteq{\cal P}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>⊇</mo><msub><mi>𝒫</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{n}\supseteq{\cal P}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>,
defined as the normalizer of the Pauli group inside the unitary group.
Leaving open the possibility that there exists some “in-between”
group <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx6.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\cal P}_{n}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒫</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\cal P}_{n}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying</p>
<table id="A4.E22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\cal P}_{n}\subseteq{\cal P}_{n}^{\star}\subset{\cal C}_{n}\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒫</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒫</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>𝒞</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\cal P}_{n}\subseteq{\cal P}_{n}^{\star}\subset{\cal C}_{n}\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS2.SSSx6.p3.2" class="ltx_p">we conclude that the quotient space of the “Pauli” embedding is
<math id="A4.SS2.SSSx6.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{U}(n)/{\cal P}_{n}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒫</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{U}(n)/{\cal P}_{n}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the Clifford group is finite,
the intrinsic dimensions of both <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx6.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{U}(n)/{\cal P}_{n}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒫</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{U}(n)/{\cal P}_{n}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="A4.SS2.SSSx6.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{U}(n)/{\cal P}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>𝒫</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{U}(n)/{\cal P}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> are equal to the dimension <math id="A4.SS2.SSSx6.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> of the unitary
group.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Overview of IDE methods tested</h2>

<section id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">lPCA :</h5>

<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since a manifold is locally isomorphic to <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the directions normal to the hyperplanes have zero variance. Given <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> nearest-neighbor (NN) of a point sampled from <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, singular value decomposition (SVD) of the <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\times D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\times D</annotation></semantics></math> matrix is performed to determine the principal components. The intrinsic dimension of the manifold is then estimated by different means:</p>
<ul id="A5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">‘maxgap’ : the component showing the biggest spectral gap is returned as the intrinsic dimension, i.e.</p>
<table id="A5.E23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{d_{i}}(x;k)=\text{argmax}_{j\in 1,\dots,\min(D,k)-1}\frac{e_{j-1}}{e_{j}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>argmax</mtext><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{d_{i}}(x;k)=\text{argmax}_{j\in 1,\dots,\min(D,k)-1}\frac{e_{j-1}}{e_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">‘ratio’ : the minimum number of components needed to explain <math id="A5.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> of the total variance.</p>
<table id="A5.E24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{d_{i}}(x;k,\epsilon)=\min\{j:R_{j}\geq 1-\epsilon\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{d_{i}}(x;k,\epsilon)=\min\{j:R_{j}\geq 1-\epsilon\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.I1.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A5.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{j}:=\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{j}\sigma^{2}_{[i]}}{\sum_{i=1}^{N}\sigma^{2}_{[i]}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>j</mi></msubsup></mstyle><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mstyle><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{j}:=\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{j}\sigma^{2}_{[i]}}{\sum_{i=1}^{N}\sigma^{2}_{[i]}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the cumulative variance ratio (<math id="A5.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(.)_{[i]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(.)_{[i]}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes that the quantity is sorted from largest to smallest).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">‘fo’ : the index <math id="A5.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> for which the (sorted) eigenvalues cross <math id="A5.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> of the largest eigenvalue.</p>
<table id="A5.E25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{d_{i}}(x;k,\epsilon)=\min\{j:\sigma^{2}_{[i]}\geq(1-\epsilon)\sigma^{2}_{[k]}\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{d_{i}}(x;k,\epsilon)=\min\{j:\sigma^{2}_{[i]}\geq(1-\epsilon)\sigma^{2}_{[k]}\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">It is easy to check that these definitions are equivalent with suitable choices of the hyperparameter <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> for manifolds with a <span id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">clear</span> spectral gap. In order to preserve computational resources, we
therefore report the results of the ‘maxgap’ technique in the main section, but we also report results of small-scale experiments with the ‘ratio’ and ‘fo’ versions in <a href="#A7.SS1" title="G.1 Other versions of local PCA ‣ Appendix G Additional experiments and plots ‣ Quantum-inspired Benchmark for Estimating Intrinsic Dimension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G.1</span></a> We created a custom function to compute the <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{d}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>d</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{d}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> according to Eq. <a href="#A5.E23" title="In 1st item ‣ lPCA : ‣ Appendix E Overview of IDE methods tested ‣ Quantum-inspired Benchmark for Estimating Intrinsic Dimension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a> above, closely drawing from the implementation in <span class="ltx_ref ltx_ref_self">scikit-dimension</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The following methods, namely <span class="ltx_ref ltx_ref_self">MLE</span>, <span class="ltx_ref ltx_ref_self">DANCo</span>, <span class="ltx_ref ltx_ref_self">CorrInt</span> and <span class="ltx_ref ltx_ref_self">TwoNN</span> were implemented by accessing the implementations directly from the <span id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">scikit-dimension</span> package.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">MLE : </h5>

<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a set of samples <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1},X_{2},...X_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1},X_{2},...X_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, related to a sample in lower-dimensional space <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> and equipped with a smooth density <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, one considers the Possion process <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda(t)</annotation></semantics></math> of number of points in a small sphere around the point with radius <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. The log-likelihood of this process (assuming a constant density <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)</annotation></semantics></math> in the sphere of radius <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\geq t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\geq t</annotation></semantics></math>) yields</p>
<table id="A5.E26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{d_{i}}(x;R)={\left[\frac{1}{N_{R}(x)}\sum_{j=1}^{N_{R}(x)}\log\frac{R}{T_{j}(x)}\right]}^{-1}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></munderover><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mi>R</mi><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{d_{i}}(x;R)={\left[\frac{1}{N_{R}(x)}\sum_{j=1}^{N_{R}(x)}\log\frac{R}{T_{j}(x)}\right]}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{j}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{j}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is the Euclidean distance between <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and its <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th NN. Some results on the statistics of these log distances were considered too.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Reference <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Haro et al. (2008)</a></cite> goes one step further and considers a noisy translation of the observed distances, which leads to a non-linear recursive equation, which they can solve self-consistently. In particular for isotropic Gaussian noise with scale <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, their ID estimate reads</p>
<table id="A5.E27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{d_{i}}(x;R)=\left[\frac{1}{N_{R}(x)}\sum_{j=1}^{N_{R}(x)}\frac{\int_{r=0}^{R}\exp(-\frac{(T_{i}-r)^{2}}{2\sigma^{2}})\log(\frac{T_{k}}{r})dr}{\int_{r=0}^{R}\exp(-\frac{(T_{i}-r)^{2}}{2\sigma^{2}})dr}\right]^{-1}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></munderover><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>R</mi></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mi>r</mi></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>R</mi></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{d_{i}}(x;R)=\left[\frac{1}{N_{R}(x)}\sum_{j=1}^{N_{R}(x)}\frac{\int_{r=0}^{R}\exp(-\frac{(T_{i}-r)^{2}}{2\sigma^{2}})\log(\frac{T_{k}}{r})dr}{\int_{r=0}^{R}\exp(-\frac{(T_{i}-r)^{2}}{2\sigma^{2}})dr}\right]^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(27)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">CorrInt : </h5>

<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For a set <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1},...,X_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1},...,X_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> of i.i.d. samples with a smooth density <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the Euclidean distance between a point <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and its <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th NN <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{k}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{k}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies</p>
<table id="A5.E28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k/n\approx f(x)V_{d_{i}}(T_{k}(x))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k/n\approx f(x)V_{d_{i}}(T_{k}(x))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(28)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{d_{i}}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{d_{i}}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> is the volume of a <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional sphere of radius <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{d_{i}}(R)\propto R^{d_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{d_{i}}(R)\propto R^{d_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the number of points <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> in a ball of radius <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> grows exponentially with <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is the motivation behind the fractal dimension definition.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">One computes the correlation integtral (sum) as</p>
<table id="A5.E29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(r)=\frac{1}{N(N-1)}\sum_{i&gt;j}\mathbf{1}_{||\mathbf{x}_{i}-\mathbf{x}_{j}||\leq r}~." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(r)=\frac{1}{N(N-1)}\sum_{i&gt;j}\mathbf{1}_{||\mathbf{x}_{i}-\mathbf{x}_{j}||\leq r}~.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(29)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">One can then estimate the dimension as</p>
<table id="A5.E30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{d_{i}}(x;k_{1},k_{2})=\frac{\log(C(r_{2})/C(r_{1}))}{\log(r_{2}/r_{1})}~," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0.330em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{d_{i}}(x;k_{1},k_{2})=\frac{\log(C(r_{2})/C(r_{1}))}{\log(r_{2}/r_{1})}~,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(30)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where the hyper-parameters <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are used to find the median distances <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">TwoNN : </h5>

<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For a constant density <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> around a point <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, the volume of the hyper-spherical shell between <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i+1</annotation></semantics></math>-th NN is drawn from an exponential distribution in the volume <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\nu_{l}=\omega_{d_{i}}(r^{d_{i}}_{l}-r^{d_{i}}_{l-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>l</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\nu_{l}=\omega_{d_{i}}(r^{d_{i}}_{l}-r^{d_{i}}_{l-1})</annotation></semantics></math>. Define <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=\frac{\Delta\nu_{2}}{\Delta\nu_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=\frac{\Delta\nu_{2}}{\Delta\nu_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> and then it follows that <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(R)=(1+R)^{-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(R)=(1+R)^{-2}</annotation></semantics></math>. It then follows that <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\mu)={d_{i}}\mu^{-{d_{i}}-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\mu)={d_{i}}\mu^{-{d_{i}}-1}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mu=\frac{r_{2}}{r_{1}}\in[1,\inf)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">inf</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=\frac{r_{2}}{r_{1}}\in[1,\inf)</annotation></semantics></math>. (Basically note that <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\mu)=f(R)\frac{dR}{d\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\mu)=f(R)\frac{dR}{d\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=\mu^{d_{i}}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>μ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=\mu^{d_{i}}-1</annotation></semantics></math>) To make matters less prone to computational errors, discard <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> of the largest <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{r_{2}}{r_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{r_{2}}{r_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> values. Thus the ID estimate is given by</p>
<table id="A5.E31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{d}_{i}(x;k,\alpha)=-\frac{\log(1-F(\mu))}{\log\mu}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>d</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{d}_{i}(x;k,\alpha)=-\frac{\log(1-F(\mu))}{\log\mu}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(31)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> represents the ratio <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{r_{2}}{r_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{r_{2}}{r_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> for the <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>NNs of the particular point.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">DANCo : </h5>

<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For a manifold <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>, consider an embedding <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:\mathbb{R}^{d_{i}}\to\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:\mathbb{R}^{d_{i}}\to\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math> which is locally isometric, smooth and possibly non-linear. Then the points in a local neighborhood are drawn uniformly from the hyperspheres. The distribution for distances for the unit hypersphere normalized by the distance of the <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th NN follows the distribution</p>
<table id="A5.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(r;k,d_{i})=kd_{i}r^{d_{i}-1}(1-r^{d_{i}})^{k-1}~," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(r;k,d_{i})=kd_{i}r^{d_{i}-1}(1-r^{d_{i}})^{k-1}~,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">while the mutual angles follow the von Mises-Fisher (VMF) distribution</p>
<table id="A5.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(\mathbf{x};\bf{\nu},\tau)=C_{d_{i}}(\tau)\exp(\tau\bf{\nu}^{T}\mathbf{x})~," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><msub><mi>𝐝</mi><mi>𝐢</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ν</mi><mi>𝐓</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.380em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(\mathbf{x};\bf{\nu},\tau)=C_{d_{i}}(\tau)\exp(\tau\bf{\nu}^{T}\mathbf{x})~,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{d_{i}}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{d_{i}}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> is a normalization constant. It should be noted that the parameter <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> in the VMF distribution denotes the concentration of angles around the mean — the parameter <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=0</annotation></semantics></math> reducing this distribution to the uniform distribution on the sphere. The joint distribution of the normalized distance and mutual angles factorizes into the product of marginals for the unit hypersphere. The ID is then estimated by minimizing the KL-divergence between the theoretical and experimental joint distribution of normalized distance and mutual angles,</p>
<table id="A5.E32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{d}_{i}(x;k)=\text{argmin}_{d=1,\dots,d_{a}}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}d\theta\int_{0}^{1}drh_{d}(r,\theta)\log(\frac{\hat{h}_{d}(r,\theta)}{h_{d}(r,\theta)})~," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>d</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>argmin</mtext><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mi>π</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo rspace="0.330em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{d}_{i}(x;k)=\text{argmin}_{d=1,\dots,d_{a}}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}d\theta\int_{0}^{1}drh_{d}(r,\theta)\log(\frac{\hat{h}_{d}(r,\theta)}{h_{d}(r,\theta)})~,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(32)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{h}</annotation></semantics></math> refers to the experimental joint distribution, and <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(r,\theta)=g(r)\cdot q(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(r,\theta)=g(r)\cdot q(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> is the theoretical joint distribution.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px6" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">ABID : </h5>

<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As discussed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">Thordsen &amp; Schubert (2022)</a></cite>, the distribution of pairwise cosines between two points drawn randomly and uniformly from a <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-ball (excluding the origin; also holds for any such spherical distribution) follows a Beta distribution on the interval <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-1,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-1,1]</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="A5.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\cos\theta)=\frac{1}{2}B(\frac{1+\cos\theta}{2};\frac{d_{i}-1}{2},\frac{d_{i}-1}{2})~," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo rspace="0.330em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\cos\theta)=\frac{1}{2}B(\frac{1+\cos\theta}{2};\frac{d_{i}-1}{2},\frac{d_{i}-1}{2})~,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">from which it follows that</p>
<table id="A5.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\cos^{2}\theta]=d_{i}^{-1}~." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>cos</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\cos^{2}\theta]=d_{i}^{-1}~.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.3" class="ltx_p">This motivates the following definition for the ABID ID estimator</p>
<table id="A5.E33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{d}_{i}(x;k)=(\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x}_{i},\mathbf{x}_{j}\sim B_{k}(x)}[\cos^{2}(\mathbf{x}_{i},\mathbf{x}_{j})])^{-1}~," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>d</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>cos</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{d}_{i}(x;k)=(\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x}_{i},\mathbf{x}_{j}\sim B_{k}(x)}[\cos^{2}(\mathbf{x}_{i},\mathbf{x}_{j})])^{-1}~,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(33)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{k}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{k}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the ball containing the kNN of x, i.e. a ball of radius <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{k}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{k}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>Comparison with other benchmarks</h2>

<div id="A6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here we present results of comparing our manifolds against other standard manifolds. In Appx <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Comparison with other benchmarks ‣ Quantum-inspired Benchmark for Estimating Intrinsic Dimension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>, we present evidence that our manifolds are also adversarial as compared to other standard benchmarks.
One of our main considerations was to compare these manifolds at fixed parameters and resources, hence we only include manifolds for which we can tune the <math id="A6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{a}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is the reason why we exclude some manifolds like the Moebius strip, torus, Swiss rolls, etc.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A6.F7" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.01335v1/new_compare_rel_gaussian.png" id="A6.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:439/365;" width="439" height="365" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span>Relative performance of IDE methods on QuIIEst manifolds and the manifold of normal isotropic Gaussian vectors . N/A indicates that the method performs very well on <math id="A6.F7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A6.F8" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.01335v1/new_compare_rel_affine.png" id="A6.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:439/365;" width="439" height="365" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span>Relative performance of IDE methods on QuIIEst manifolds and the manifold of affine linear nullspace <math id="A6.F8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>. N/A indicates that the method performs very well on <math id="A6.F8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A6.F9" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.01335v1/new_compare_rel_mbeta.png" id="A6.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:439/366;" width="439" height="366" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 9: </span>Relative performance of IDE methods on QuIIEst manifolds and the manifold <math id="A6.F9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>. N/A indicates that the method performs very well on <math id="A6.F9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Isotropic Gaussian vectors</h5>

<div id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Based on <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hein &amp; Audibert (2005)</a></cite>, we compare our manifolds against isotropic Gaussian vectors <math id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> linearly embedded into <math id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d_{a}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d_{a}}</annotation></semantics></math>, cf. Fig. <a href="#A6.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ Appendix F Comparison with other benchmarks ‣ Quantum-inspired Benchmark for Estimating Intrinsic Dimension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Affine spaces</h5>

<div id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Affine spaces are isomorphic to the linear nullspaces we considered in our main text. Given <math id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i},d_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i},d_{a}</annotation></semantics></math>, the nullspace of matrix <math id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{a}\times d_{a}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{a}\times d_{a}}</annotation></semantics></math> with rank <math id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{a}-d_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{a}-d_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of a hyperplane of intrinsic dimension <math id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. In order to preserve the "smoothness" of our manifold, we sample the coefficients of the basis vectors of the nullspace from the standard unit normal, cf. Fig‘<a href="#A6.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ Appendix F Comparison with other benchmarks ‣ Quantum-inspired Benchmark for Estimating Intrinsic Dimension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Nonlinear manifolds</h5>

<div id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Based on <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rozza et al. (2012)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Campadelli et al. (2015)</a></cite>, we use a generalized version of the manifolds denoted by <math id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> — where we uniformly sample from <math id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="X:[0,1)^{d_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X:[0,1)^{d_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>, construct <math id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=\sin(\cos(2\pi X))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=\sin(\cos(2\pi X))</annotation></semantics></math>, and finally linearly embed it into <math id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d_{a}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d_{a}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This differs from the original formulation in that they append a <math id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{Y}=\cos(\sin(2\pi X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{Y}=\cos(\sin(2\pi X)</annotation></semantics></math> and finally duplicate this to get <math id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{a}=4d_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{a}=4d_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, cf. Fig‘<a href="#A6.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ Appendix F Comparison with other benchmarks ‣ Quantum-inspired Benchmark for Estimating Intrinsic Dimension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the purposes of this experiment, we run extensive scaling experiments by choosing small-dimensional manifolds. In particular we choose Grassmanians with ID of 2,3,4,5; Stiefels with ID of 3,5; Flags with ID of 4,12; and Pauli with ID of 3.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A7" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix G </span>Additional experiments and plots</h2>

<section id="A7.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">G.1 </span>Other versions of local PCA</h3>

<figure id="A7.F10" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01335v1/lpca_ex.svg" id="A7.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:537/558;" width="537" height="558"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 10: </span>lPCA maxgap compared to lPCA ratio and FO versions. ‘FO’ usually shows a quicker convergence to lPCA maxgap value as compared to the ‘ratio’ version.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A7.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We observe that the <math id="A7.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{d}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>d</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{d}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> from lPCA vary significantly on the hyperparameter <math id="A7.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>; in particular this seems to suggest that there is no clear spectral gap in the singular values of the data covariance matrix <math id="A7.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="XX^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">XX^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, we do note that there always exist some value of <math id="A7.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> for which <math id="A7.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta(\epsilon^{*})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta(\epsilon^{*})=0</annotation></semantics></math>, but there is no clear pattern or consistent value for the choice of <math id="A7.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> even for distinct versions for the same manifold, atleast within the scope of the experiments performed. We thus relegate this interesting investigation to a future project. The results are plotted in Fig. <a href="#A7.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ G.1 Other versions of local PCA ‣ Appendix G Additional experiments and plots ‣ Quantum-inspired Benchmark for Estimating Intrinsic Dimension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A7.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">G.2 </span>Squeezing : comparison with spheres</h3>

<figure id="A7.F11" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01335v1/distort_spheres_compare.svg" id="A7.F11.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:537/322;" width="537" height="322"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 11: </span>Comparison of errors when distorting QuIIEst manifolds versus spheres. The large change indicates that spheres become drastically more difficult to do IDE on with increasing distortion, as opposed to QuIIEst manifolds. </figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A7.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present in Fig <a href="#S5.F3" title='Figure 3 ‣ 5.3 Anisotropic distortions : "Squeezing" ‣ 5 Results ‣ Quantum-inspired Benchmark for Estimating Intrinsic Dimension' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> the effect of squeezing for spheres, as compared to QuIIEst manifolds. In particular, we observe that spheres exhibit a very large increase in the error rate as anisotropy is increased. In conjunction with Section <a href="#S5.SS3" title='5.3 Anisotropic distortions : "Squeezing" ‣ 5 Results ‣ Quantum-inspired Benchmark for Estimating Intrinsic Dimension' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>, this demonstrates that QuIIEst manifolds are already challenging enough for the IDE methods tested, and enhances it applicability as a more robust performance evaluator for IDE.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A7.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">G.3 </span>Relative error as a function of ID</h3>

<figure id="A7.F12" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01335v1/app_dim_scaling.svg" id="A7.F12.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:537/128;" width="537" height="128"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 12: </span>Scaling of relative error with ID for the other manifolds. We observe the same sort of trend as discussed in the main text, except for flag, which has limited resolution.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A7.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We include here some other plots on scaling of the relative error for different manifolds in Fig <a href="#A7.F12" title="Figure 12 ‣ G.3 Relative error as a function of ID ‣ Appendix G Additional experiments and plots ‣ Quantum-inspired Benchmark for Estimating Intrinsic Dimension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>. We once again notice the trend mentioned in the main text, where an initial over-estimation gives way to an under-estimation. This trend is not immediately obvious for the Flag manifolds due to lack of sufficient points. The Pauli manifold is excluded since we could only test 4 different IDs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A7.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">G.4 </span>Scaling with sample size</h3>

<div id="A7.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The dependence of the intrinsic dimension estimate <math id="A7.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{d}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>d</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{d}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> comes from the fact that the fraction of neighbors in a small neighborhood for manifolds are given by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Levina &amp; Bickel (2004)</a></cite></p>
</div>
<div id="A7.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A7.E34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A7.E34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{k}{N}=\Omega_{d_{i}}\rho(x)T_{k}^{d_{i}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>k</mi><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{k}{N}=\Omega_{d_{i}}\rho(x)T_{k}^{d_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(34)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A7.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Thus in order to get a uniform and dense sampling of points, we need to hold <math id="A7.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k/(T_{k}^{d_{i}}N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k/(T_{k}^{d_{i}}N)</annotation></semantics></math> fixed, which translates to the condition that</p>
<table id="A7.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A7.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\propto\exp(-d_{i})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\propto\exp(-d_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A7.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We investigate the effect of changing <math id="A7.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> while holding hyper-parameters fixed. We logarithmically sample <math id="A7.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="A7.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N/d_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N/d_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> goes from 2 to 300. The results are summarized in Fig <a href="#A7.F13" title="Figure 13 ‣ G.4 Scaling with sample size ‣ Appendix G Additional experiments and plots ‣ Quantum-inspired Benchmark for Estimating Intrinsic Dimension" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a></p>
</div>
<figure id="A7.F13" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01335v1/sample_scaling_rel.svg" id="A7.F13.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:537/287;" width="537" height="287"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 13: </span>Performance of IDE methods as a function of <math id="A7.F13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N/d_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N/d_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. We observe a gradual convergence with increasing sample size. The shaded region shows the 1-<math id="A7.F13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> error in the local ID estimates, averaged over three seeds.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A7.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">G.5 </span>Correlation of IDE performance with Tr <math id="A7.SS5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚺</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A7.SS5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle R^{2}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle R^{2}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="A7.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present the results for the correlation of IDE performance with Tr <math id="A7.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚺</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A7.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle R^{2}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle R^{2}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> here. Most methods seem to show no dependence on the total variance, with few exceptions being DANCo for linear nullspaces. On the other hand, there is a weak positive correlation between performance and inter-component correlation. The latter makes <span id="A7.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">a posteriori</span> sense since this implies that the manifold embeddings show structrual similarites, which makes it easier
for the methods to discover the latent dimension.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A7.F14" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A7.F14.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01335v1/data_cov_ide_tr_sigma.svg" id="A7.F14.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/255;" width="548" height="255"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span>Tr <math id="A7.F14.sf1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚺</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A7.F14.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01335v1/data_cov_ide_rsq.svg" id="A7.F14.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/254;" width="548" height="254"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span><math id="A7.F14.sf2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle R^{2}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle R^{2}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 14: </span>IDE performance seems to be almost independent for Tr <math id="A7.F14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚺</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> with slight dependence observed for the angle-based methods. On the other hand, most methods except lPCA, seem to show weak positive correlation with performance and <math id="A7.F14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle R^{2}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle R^{2}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>. The light vertical maroon lines represent the corresponding quantities for different classes in MNIST. </figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A7.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">G.6 </span>Geometric Properties</h3>

